TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2) – Space Mission Design","overview":"Students design a preliminary space mission benefiting Australia, covering the space segment: mission purpose, spacecraft count, users, launch site and vehicle, orbit and orbital elements, manoeuvres, ground control and communications, mission duration and end-of-life. Design may use STK, PIGI or approved software, supplemented by hand calculations, and is reported as a concise technical report with supporting screenshots and software files."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale - Include the following required information: • What is the spacecraft's primary mission? • Why is the mission needed? • How many spacecraft are used and why? • Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design - Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate - Include relevant screenshots and software files (in the main body of the report and/or appendix) - Include the following information: • Where it will launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? • What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? • Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? • Which ground control facilities will you use? • How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? • How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (eg. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"Mission Design"},{"id":"R3","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R5","met":"partial","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R6","met":"yes","where":"Launch"},{"id":"R7","met":"yes","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R8","met":"partial","where":"Orbit; Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R9","met":"yes","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R10","met":"yes","where":"Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R11","met":"partial","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R12","met":"partial","where":"Orbit; Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R13","met":"yes","where":"Executive Summary; Introduction; Mission Design; Conclusion; Bibliography"},{"id":"R14","met":"partial","where":"Orbit; Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R15","met":"partial","where":"Bibliography"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Orbit","issue":"The statement that the spacecraft has a constant supply of sunlight is incorrect: sun-synchronous orbit fixes local solar time of node passage but does not eliminate orbital eclipses."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"A sun-synchronous orbit does not pass over almost the entire Earth in the broad sense implied; its ground-track latitude coverage is limited by its near-polar inclination, and revisit of a specific reef is not established."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"The listed RAAN of 27.33° is not determined by the launch facility's location. Launch latitude constrains achievable inclination; RAAN depends on launch timing and desired orbital-plane orientation."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"The claim that the circular orbit has no perigee is imprecise: perigee is not uniquely defined for a circular orbit, as are argument of perigee and true anomaly; argument of latitude is a valid substitute when inclination is non-equatorial."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"The access report contains a duplicated access entry and apparent ordering/date inconsistencies, so its claimed access frequency and mean duration need validation. Contact is not guaranteed for the vast majority of passes merely because the orbit is near-polar."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"A drag sail at 600 km may reduce orbital lifetime, but deorbit time and compliance with disposal requirements are not estimated. The report also says the satellite has no thrusters, so the claimed EOL manoeuvre should be described as passive decay rather than an orbital manoeuvre."},{"where":"Launch","issue":"Vega E was a future, unbuilt launcher in the report's stated timeframe; the proposed 2025 ride is speculative and does not establish a confirmed launch opportunity or compatibility with the required orbit."},{"where":"Introduction","issue":"The report's claim that ocean acidity has increased by 30 percent since the industrial revolution is not clearly defined or supported as written; ocean surface acidity change is commonly expressed as an increase in acidity rather than this unsupported percentage."}],"overall":"A coherent preliminary mission concept with useful STK visualisations and a stated orbit, ground station, and lifetime. The design remains only partially substantiated: launch feasibility, actual reef revisit/imaging performance, communications analysis, and EOL decay are not resolved. Presentation and referencing also need improvement.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Accomplished","mark":7.0,"scale":10.0,"points":7.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"Identifies the reef-monitoring mission, explains the Australian need, justifies using one satellite, and names likely users and funding sources. The rationale is relevant, though customer/operator roles are not clearly separated and the discussion would benefit from stronger, more specific sourcing.","evidence":[{"where":"Introduction","quote":"The proposed satellite would likely be used by governmental and science agencies (e.g. CSIRO, BOM) for research and/or education."},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"Thus, only one satellite is required."}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":30.0,"max":60.0,"reasoning":"Provides a plausible mission concept, orbit elements, STK access results, ground facility, five-year duration, and an EOL concept. However, launch selection rests on speculative future rideshare assumptions; operational feasibility and data needs are thinly supported. The orbit discussion contains misleading claims about continuous sunlight and broad coverage, while EOL disposal is not quantified or demonstrated. Software files are not included in the submission.","evidence":[{"where":"Launch","quote":"The proposed satellite will thus use the Vega E rocket as a launch vehicle – launched from the Guiana Space Centre Spaceport."},{"where":"Orbit","quote":"Specifically, an altitude of 600km requires a mean inclination of $ 97.7924^{\\circ} $ for the satellite to remain sun-synchronous."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"An average contact duration of almost ten minutes was achieved"},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"A span of five years is a sound estimate of the useful lifespan of the proposed satellite."}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":5.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"Summarises key design choices and acknowledges launch uncertainty, but conclusions are largely restatement and do not critically assess whether the design meets imaging, data-return, or disposal needs. The illumination claim is overstated: an SSO does not ensure uninterrupted sunlight on the spacecraft.","evidence":[{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"A polar sun-synchronous orbit allows for consistent illumination of the oceans while maintaining a constant sun angle."}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":10.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"Has a recognisable report structure, executive summary, figures, tables, and a bibliography. Presentation is weakened by inconsistent labelling and formatting, grammatical errors, incomplete reference details, and use of Wikipedia as a source. Some tables and figure captions are difficult to interpret.","evidence":[{"where":"Executive Summary","quote":"This report outlines the intended designs and procedures involved in the construction of a Earth observation satellite"},{"where":"Bibliography","quote":"[6] https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_in_spaceflight. [Online]."}]}]}
